      "id": "archogen_four_forms",
      "input": "; examples/s0-heartbeat/system.eadl \u2014 the S0 base fixture.\n;\n; ROADMAP.md \u00a712 S0 asks for \"a roughly twenty-line feature-only description\" whose generated\n; hosted executable \"instantiates one simple service and produces a defined event\". This is it:\n; one output device, one service, one platform, two periodic tasks \u2014 sixteen lines of\n; declarations.\n;\n; WHY TWO TASKS AND NOT ONE. The observation horizon is the system's hyperperiod, and with a\n; single task that is one period, so the trace is one event and no parameter change can produce\n; a meaningfully different observation. Two is the smallest task set for which the S0 gate's\n; changed-input case can say anything.\n;\n; WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT. No execution bound, no entry point, no stack size \u2014 \u00a77.3 puts\n; those in the build manifest and the engine. Also no message text and no run length: the event\n; format and the decision to observe exactly one hyperperiod are the engine's, and writing them\n; here would be \"embedding a prebuilt output without consuming the functional input\", which \u00a712\n; S0 states does not satisfy F28.\n;\n; The expected observation is frozen in expected/system.txt and was written before any emitter\n; existed. The observation contract it rests on is in README.md.\n\n(defblock console.uart\n  (offers (observable-output true)))\n\n(defplatform host.playground\n  (offers (core-count 1 tick))\n  (requires (uses console.uart)))\n\n(defservice console.write\n  (requires (needs observable-output)))\n\n(defsystem heartbeat\n  (task beat\n    (period 10 ms) (deadline 10 ms) (deadline-from release)\n    (priority 1) (uses console.write) (on-overrun fault))\n  (task chime\n    (period 30 ms) (deadline 30 ms) (deadline-from release)\n    (priority 2) (uses console.write) (on-overrun fault))\n  (requires (uses console.write))\n  (platform (uses host.playground)))\n",
